=== TABLE FOOTBALL FEEDBACK SYSTEM — RASPBERRY PI SERVER v3 ===

Build a Raspberry Pi 4 server that acts as the central hub for a table
football feedback project. It runs as a WiFi hotspot, MQTT broker, Flask
web server, and SQLite database, starting automatically on boot.

=== SYSTEM CONTEXT ===

Physical setup per table:
  - Side A Pico 2W: RFID + display + WiFi + I2C slave (ball tracker)
  - Side B Pico 2W: RFID + display + WiFi + I2C slave (ball tracker)
  - BOTH Picos receive ball tracking data from the same I2C wire.
  - Both Picos use ball data for their own local display.
  - ONLY the primary Pico (role="primary") publishes ball_position MQTT
    messages to the Pi. The secondary Pico discards ball data after
    rendering its local display.
  - Only the primary Pico publishes game state (score, GAME_OVER).
  - Secondary Pico only publishes: RFID scans and heartbeat.
  - Both Picos can operate STANDALONE without the Pi (offline mode).
  - When Pi is available, Picos connect to its hotspot and use MQTT.
  - When Pi is unreachable, Picos use locally stored player names from
    flash, run the game, but save no history.

The Raspberry Pi 4:
  - WiFi hotspot at 192.168.4.1, SSID: "TableFootball", WPA2
  - DHCP range: 192.168.4.10 – 192.168.4.100
  - Mosquitto MQTT broker on port 1883
  - Flask server on port 5000
  - SQLite database (WAL mode)
  - All services auto-start on boot via systemd

Users open http://192.168.4.1:5000 on their phone or laptop.

=== TECH STACK ===

  Server:
    - Python 3.11+
    - Flask 3.x (threaded=True)
    - paho-mqtt 2.x
    - SQLite 3 (WAL mode, foreign keys enforced)
    - python-dotenv
    - NO ORM — raw SQL through database.py only

  Frontend:
    - Plain HTML, CSS, JavaScript
    - Chart.js from CDN (player profile page only)
    - Server-Sent Events for all real-time updates
    - SSE fallback: poll /api/tables every 5 seconds if SSE drops
    - Ball position shown as moving dot on CSS field visualization
    - Ball position is NEVER stored in the database — broadcast only

  Real-time transport:
    - Pico → Pi: MQTT (QoS 1 for RFID/state, QoS 0 for ball position)
    - Pi → browser: SSE (one-way push, no libraries needed)
    - Ball position: QoS 0 MQTT → SSE → browser (lossy is fine at 10Hz)
    - WebRTC: NOT used
    - UDP: NOT used

